1.3 Where AI fits
AI changes the workflow, not the goal. The goal is still to communicate honestly with evidence. AI simply gives us new tools for drafting, coding, revising, critiquing, and exploring alternatives.
In this course, we will use AI in ways that make our thinking more visible. For example, you might ask an AI tool to:

suggest three possible headlines for a chart;
explain a confusing error message in your code;
identify places where a paragraph sounds vague;
generate possible audience questions about your draft;
translate technical language into plain language;
propose alternative visual forms for a comparison.

Those are useful starting points. They are not finished work. You still need to check the data, inspect the code, verify factual claims, make design choices, and decide what belongs in the final product.

The AI use log
For major assignments, you will keep a short AI use log. The goal is not to punish AI use. The goal is to make your workflow transparent and to help you notice when AI helped, when it failed, and when your own judgment changed the final product.
At minimum, an AI use log should record:

what tool you used;
what you asked it to do;
what output was useful;
what output you rejected or revised;
how you verified the final result.


Working rule: If AI affected the final product, document how. If AI suggested something you did not use, you do not need a long essay, but you should understand why you rejected it.

