ExampleExample 3.1: Source file versus output file



If you write analysis.qmd, that file is the editable source. When you render it, Quarto might create analysis.html, analysis.pdf, or analysis.docx.
The source file is like a recipe. The rendered file is the meal your audience sees. If you want to change the final product, you usually edit the source file and render again.

3.9 AI and Quarto
AI tools can be helpful when learning Quarto syntax. They can draft YAML headers, suggest image syntax, troubleshoot an error message, or convert a rough outline into a starter .qmd file.
But AI tools can also invent options, mix syntax from R Markdown, Markdown, HTML, LaTeX, and Quarto, or suggest code that looks plausible but does not render. Treat AI output as a draft, not as proof.
Responsible AI use with Quarto includes:

rendering the file yourself;
reading error messages;
checking that the output matches the source;
verifying that images, equations, and links work;
documenting AI help when course instructions require it.







TipTest AI-generated Quarto by rendering



If AI gives you Quarto syntax, the render is the first test. If it does not render, the syntax is not ready. If it renders but communicates the wrong thing, the communication is still not ready.

4.3 What counts as a data source?
A data source is not only a spreadsheet. It is the full package of materials that explain where the data came from, what the rows and columns mean, and how the data may be used.
A usable data source often includes:

the data file or table;
documentation or metadata;
a data dictionary or codebook;
methodology notes;
date of collection or publication;
source owner or organization;
license or terms of use;
update schedule;
contact information or citation guidance.







NoteData file is not the whole source



If you download only a .csv file and ignore the documentation, you may not know what the variables mean, what population is included, how missing values are coded, or whether the data may be reused.

4.9 Using AI to find and evaluate sources
AI can help with source discovery, but it should not be trusted as the source itself.
Useful AI tasks:

brainstorm search terms;
suggest possible official agencies or data portals;
summarize documentation you provide;
identify variables that may matter for a question;
draft a source note from information you verified;
suggest questions to ask about a dataset.

Risky AI tasks:

asking AI to invent a dataset;
trusting a link without opening it;
accepting a license summary without checking the source page;
asking AI to decide whether a dataset proves a causal claim;
pasting private or identifiable data into an unapproved tool.







TipAI source rule



Use AI to help you search and think. Do not cite AI as the data source. Cite the dataset, organization, documentation, or publication that you actually inspected.

5.12 AI and variable meaning
AI tools can help draft a data dictionary, but they can also invent definitions.
Useful AI tasks:

turn rough notes into a cleaner data dictionary table;
suggest questions to ask about unclear variables;
identify variable names that may need definitions;
draft audience-friendly labels from definitions you provide;
check whether a claim uses a variable more strongly than the definition supports.

Risky AI tasks:

asking AI to define columns without documentation;
accepting guessed category meanings;
letting AI recode missing values without checking the source;
using AI-generated code when you do not understand the row meaning;
asking AI to infer sensitive categories from names or identifiers.







WarningVerify AI-generated definitions



If AI suggests a variable definition, treat it as a draft question, not as an answer. Verify definitions against the data dictionary, source documentation, codebook, or source owner.
